{"meta": {"course": "statics", "chapter": "01", "section": "05", "chapter_title": "Force Vectors", "title": "Module 5: Force Vectors \u2014 2D, 3D, Dot and Cross Products", "description": "Build the vector toolbox that every later statics topic depends on: 2D resolution, 3D unit vectors via position vectors, the dot product for projection and angles, and the cross product for moments.", "learning_objectives": ["Resolve a 2D force into x and y components without quadrant errors.", "Build a 3D force vector from a position vector along its line of action using direction cosines or unit vectors.", "Use the dot product to compute angles between vectors and projections along a line.", "Use the cross product to compute the moment of a force about a point and to test collinearity."], "concept_ids": ["force_vector", "force_components", "unit_vector", "direction_cosines", "dot_product", "cross_product"], "prereq_section_ids": ["statics/01/01"], "practice_ids": ["force_vec_2d_resultant", "force_vec_3d_unit", "force_vec_dot_projection", "force_vec_cross_anticommute"], "estimated_time_minutes": 13, "order": 5}, "content": "# Force Vectors\n\n> **\"Vectors are the language in which the laws of mechanics are most naturally written.\"**\n> - J. L. Meriam, *Engineering Mechanics: Statics*\n\n---\n\n## Opening: A Tower That Stayed Up Because the Math Was Right\n\nWhen the 553 m CN Tower was built in Toronto, its tall mast section was lifted and braced by guy cables anchored at angled ground points. Each cable pulled on the mast in a fully three-dimensional direction, and the riggers could not simply add the cable forces as numbers. A cable pulling 200 kN toward the north-east and one pulling 200 kN toward the south-east do not produce 400 kN of restraint; depending on the angle between them they might produce far less, and in the worst case nearly zero net horizontal hold. Getting the **directions** right, not just the magnitudes, was the difference between a stable lift and a buckled mast.\n\nThis is the core idea of the whole course in miniature. Before equilibrium, moments, trusses, or friction make any sense, a force has to be treated as a vector: a quantity with both size and direction that obeys vector addition. Treating a force as a plain number with a direction loosely attached is the single most common source of avoidable error in statics.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Every later topic in statics, equilibrium, moments, structural analysis, friction, runs on top of one prerequisite skill: representing and combining forces as vectors. Beer, Johnston, Mazurek, Cornwell, and Self give force vectors their own chapter (\"Statics of Particles\") before any equilibrium discussion, and Hibbeler does the same in his Chapter 2. The ordering is deliberate.\n\nThis module covers the four force-vector skills you will use in every later problem: **2D resolution, 3D resolution with direction cosines and unit vectors, the dot product, and the cross product.**\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Explain** why a force is a vector and why magnitudes alone cannot be added\n2. **Resolve** a 2D force into rectangular components and reassemble a resultant from components\n3. **Build** a 3D force vector from a line of action using a position vector and unit vector\n4. **Use** the dot product to find the angle between two lines and the projection of a force onto a direction\n5. **Use** the cross product to compute the moment of a force and to test for parallel or perpendicular directions\n\n---\n\n## Forces Are Vectors, Not Scalars\n\nA force has both a magnitude and a direction. Two 100 N forces along the same line add to 200 N. Two 100 N forces at 90 degrees add to about 141 N. Two 100 N forces at 180 degrees add to zero. Adding magnitudes alone discards the direction information that decides the result.\n\nVectors are added head-to-tail (the parallelogram rule). For more than two forces, build a polygon. The closed polygon corresponds to a system in equilibrium, a fact that becomes the geometric heart of the next module.\n\nA vector is also unchanged by translation along its line of action when applied to a particle. This is the **principle of transmissibility** for particles. (For rigid bodies a similar principle holds, with the qualification that the line of action must be preserved.)\n\n| Quantity | Type | What it carries |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mass, temperature, time | Scalar | Magnitude only |\n| Force, displacement, moment | Vector | Magnitude and direction |\n| Work, energy | Scalar (from a dot product) | Magnitude only |\n\n---\n\n## Resolving a 2D Force into Components\n\nMost planar problems decompose forces into x and y components. If a force $F$ has magnitude $F$ and acts at angle $\\theta$ measured counter-clockwise from the +x axis:\n\n$$F_x = F \\cos\\theta, \\quad F_y = F \\sin\\theta$$\n\nThe reverse computation reassembles components:\n\n$$F = \\sqrt{F_x^2 + F_y^2}, \\quad \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{F_y}{F_x}\\right)$$\n\nThe arctangent must be interpreted with quadrant awareness. A vector with $F_x = -3$, $F_y = -4$ has the same arctangent magnitude as $F_x = 3$, $F_y = 4$, but the angles differ by 180 degrees. Use the signs of both components to place the angle in the correct quadrant.\n\nComponent arithmetic is the safer route for adding more than two forces:\n\n$$\\vec{R} = \\sum_i \\vec{F}_i, \\quad R_x = \\sum_i F_{ix}, \\quad R_y = \\sum_i F_{iy}$$\n\nYou sum the components, then re-assemble. This avoids compounded angle errors.\n\nTwo equal forces meet at an angle. Slide that angle and watch the resultant: at 0 degrees they reinforce fully, at 180 degrees they cancel, and the magnitude follows the law of cosines, never the naive sum. This is the CN-tower lesson made interactive.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Resultant of two forces at an angle: R = sqrt(A^2 + B^2 + 2AB cos(phi))\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"A\",\"label\":\"Force A magnitude\",\"min\":0,\"max\":300,\"step\":10,\"unit\":\"N\",\"default\":100},{\"name\":\"B\",\"label\":\"Force B magnitude\",\"min\":0,\"max\":300,\"step\":10,\"unit\":\"N\",\"default\":100},{\"name\":\"phi\",\"label\":\"Angle between A and B\",\"min\":0,\"max\":180,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":90}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Naive magnitude sum A + B (wrong)\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"A + B\"},{\"label\":\"True resultant R\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"sqrt(A^2 + B^2 + 2 * A * B * cos(phi * 3.14159265 / 180))\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"phi\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n---\n\n## 3D Forces: Direction Cosines and Unit Vectors\n\nIn 3D, a force is given by its magnitude and a direction. The direction is specified either by direction cosines or by a position vector along the line of action.\n\nIf a force $F$ makes angles $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, $\\gamma$ with the +x, +y, +z axes:\n\n$$F_x = F \\cos\\alpha, \\quad F_y = F \\cos\\beta, \\quad F_z = F \\cos\\gamma$$\n\nwith the constraint $\\cos^2\\alpha + \\cos^2\\beta + \\cos^2\\gamma = 1$. The three direction cosines are the components of the **unit vector** $\\hat{u}$ along $F$.\n\nWhen the force acts along a known line, say from point A to point B, the unit vector is built from the position vector:\n\n$$\\vec{r}_{AB} = (x_B - x_A)\\hat{i} + (y_B - y_A)\\hat{j} + (z_B - z_A)\\hat{k}$$\n\n$$\\hat{u}_{AB} = \\frac{\\vec{r}_{AB}}{|\\vec{r}_{AB}|}, \\qquad \\vec{F} = F \\,\\hat{u}_{AB}$$\n\nThis is the Hibbeler workflow: write the line, build the unit vector, multiply by magnitude. It avoids the trap of guessing direction cosines from a 3D figure.\n\n---\n\n## The Dot Product: Projection and Angle Between Vectors\n\nThe dot product of two vectors is a scalar:\n\n$$\\vec{A} \\cdot \\vec{B} = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z = AB\\cos\\theta$$\n\nIt does two jobs in statics.\n\n**Angle between two lines.** Solving for the angle:\n\n$$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\vec{A} \\cdot \\vec{B}}{AB}$$\n\nThis is how you get the angle between two members at a joint, or between a force and a surface, without trigonometric guesswork.\n\n**Projection of a vector onto a line.** The component of force $\\vec{F}$ along a unit vector $\\hat{u}$ is:\n\n$$F_\\| = \\vec{F} \\cdot \\hat{u}$$\n\nWhen you need the force component parallel to a member's axis, this is the operation. The parallel component contributes axial loading; the perpendicular complement (computed by Pythagoras or by vector subtraction) contributes shear or bending.\n\nThe dot product is commutative ($\\vec{A} \\cdot \\vec{B} = \\vec{B} \\cdot \\vec{A}$) and produces zero when two vectors are perpendicular.\n\n<inline-check question=\"The dot product of two nonzero force vectors comes out to exactly zero. What does that tell you about them?\" explanation=\"Since A dot B = AB cos(theta), a zero result with nonzero magnitudes forces cos(theta) = 0, meaning theta = 90 degrees. The vectors are perpendicular, so neither has any component along the other.\" options='[{\"text\":\"They are perpendicular to each other.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"They are parallel and point the same way.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"They are equal in magnitude.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## The Cross Product: Moments and Perpendicular Direction\n\nThe cross product produces a vector:\n\n$$\\vec{A} \\times \\vec{B} = (A_y B_z - A_z B_y)\\hat{i} - (A_x B_z - A_z B_x)\\hat{j} + (A_x B_y - A_y B_x)\\hat{k}$$\n\nwith magnitude $AB\\sin\\theta$ and direction given by the right-hand rule.\n\nIn statics, the cross product is the cleanest way to compute the **moment of a force about a point**:\n\n$$\\vec{M}_O = \\vec{r} \\times \\vec{F}$$\n\nwhere $\\vec{r}$ is any position vector from the point O to any point on F's line of action. This will become Chapter 2's foundation.\n\nThe cross product is **anti-commutative**: $\\vec{A} \\times \\vec{B} = -\\vec{B} \\times \\vec{A}$. Confusing the order is the most common cross-product error and produces sign flips in moment calculations.\n\nIt is also **zero** when two vectors are parallel, a useful collinearity check.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"3d-force-resolution\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 5.1: Adding Three Coplanar Forces by Components\n\n**Problem:** Three cables pull on a ring bolt in the same plane: $F_1 = 60$ N along the +x axis, $F_2 = 80$ N at $60^\\circ$, and $F_3 = 50$ N at $135^\\circ$ (all angles measured counter-clockwise from +x). Find the magnitude and direction of the resultant.\n\n**Step 1: Resolve each force into components.**\n\n| Force | $F_x = F\\cos\\theta$ | $F_y = F\\sin\\theta$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $F_1 = 60$ at $0^\\circ$ | $+60.00$ | $0.00$ |\n| $F_2 = 80$ at $60^\\circ$ | $+40.00$ | $+69.28$ |\n| $F_3 = 50$ at $135^\\circ$ | $-35.36$ | $+35.36$ |\n\n**Step 2: Sum the components.**\n\n$$R_x = 60.00 + 40.00 - 35.36 = 64.64 \\text{ N}$$\n\n$$R_y = 0.00 + 69.28 + 35.36 = 104.64 \\text{ N}$$\n\n**Step 3: Reassemble the resultant.**\n\n$$R = \\sqrt{R_x^2 + R_y^2} = \\sqrt{64.64^2 + 104.64^2} = \\boxed{123.0 \\text{ N}}$$\n\n$$\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{104.64}{64.64}\\right) = \\boxed{58.3^\\circ}$$\n\nBoth components are positive, so the resultant lies in the first quadrant and the arctangent needs no quadrant correction. Notice the resultant (123 N) is far below the naive magnitude sum (190 N); direction is doing real work here.\n\n---\n\n### Example 5.2: From a 3D Geometry to Resolved Components\n\n**Problem:** A cable in a 3D anchor system pulls from point A(2, 0, 3) m toward point B(5, 4, 0) m with tension 250 N. Resolve the force into components.\n\n**Step 1: Position vector A to B.**\n$$\\vec{r}_{AB} = (5-2)\\hat{i} + (4-0)\\hat{j} + (0-3)\\hat{k} = 3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} - 3\\hat{k} \\text{ m}$$\n\n**Step 2: Magnitude.**\n$$|\\vec{r}_{AB}| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2 + (-3)^2} = \\sqrt{34} \\approx 5.831 \\text{ m}$$\n\n**Step 3: Unit vector.**\n$$\\hat{u}_{AB} = \\frac{3}{5.831}\\hat{i} + \\frac{4}{5.831}\\hat{j} - \\frac{3}{5.831}\\hat{k} \\approx 0.514\\hat{i} + 0.686\\hat{j} - 0.514\\hat{k}$$\n\nQuick check: $0.514^2 + 0.686^2 + 0.514^2 \\approx 0.999 \\approx 1$, so the unit vector is valid.\n\n**Step 4: Force vector.**\n$$\\vec{F} = 250 \\,\\hat{u}_{AB} \\approx \\boxed{128.6\\hat{i} + 171.5\\hat{j} - 128.6\\hat{k} \\text{ N}}$$\n\nThat is the only correct workflow. Estimating angles by eye from a 3D drawing produces 5 to 15 degree errors that propagate through every subsequent equilibrium equation.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build the 3D force vector from geometry (Example 5.2)\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The cable runs from A(2,0,3) to B(5,4,0). What is the position vector r_AB?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"3 i + 4 j - 3 k\",\"7 i + 4 j + 3 k\",\"-3 i - 4 j + 3 k\",\"3 i + 4 j + 3 k\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"r_AB = (5-2) i + (4-0) j + (0-3) k = 3 i + 4 j - 3 k. Always subtract the tail (A) from the head (B).\"},{\"prompt\":\"What is the magnitude |r_AB|?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"sqrt(34) = 5.831 m\",\"sqrt(50) = 7.071 m\",\"10 m\",\"sqrt(25) = 5 m\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"|r_AB| = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2 + (-3)^2) = sqrt(9 + 16 + 9) = sqrt(34) = 5.831 m.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The unit vector is u = r_AB / 5.831. Which is correct?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"0.514 i + 0.686 j - 0.514 k\",\"0.600 i + 0.800 j - 0.600 k\",\"0.300 i + 0.400 j - 0.300 k\",\"0.514 i + 0.514 j - 0.686 k\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"u = (3/5.831) i + (4/5.831) j - (3/5.831) k = 0.514 i + 0.686 j - 0.514 k. Check: 0.514^2 + 0.686^2 + 0.514^2 = 0.999, valid.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Multiply by the 250 N tension. What is the force vector?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"128.6 i + 171.5 j - 128.6 k N\",\"125 i + 167 j - 125 k N\",\"150 i + 200 j - 150 k N\",\"75 i + 100 j - 75 k N\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"F = 250 u = 128.6 i + 171.5 j - 128.6 k N. Write the line, build the unit vector, multiply by magnitude: that is the whole 3D workflow.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n### Example 5.3: Dot Product for Angle and Axial Projection\n\n**Problem:** Two members meet at a joint. Member 1 runs along direction $\\vec{A} = 3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} + 0\\hat{k}$ and member 2 along $\\vec{B} = 0\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k}$ (both in meters of run). (a) Find the angle between them. (b) Find the component of the 250 N cable force from Example 5.2, $\\vec{F} = 128.6\\hat{i} + 171.5\\hat{j} - 128.6\\hat{k}$ N, that acts along the line A to B.\n\n**Part (a): Angle between members.**\n\n$$\\vec{A} \\cdot \\vec{B} = (3)(0) + (4)(4) + (0)(3) = 16$$\n\n$$A = \\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = 5, \\qquad B = \\sqrt{4^2+3^2} = 5$$\n\n$$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{16}{(5)(5)} = 0.640 \\implies \\theta = \\cos^{-1}(0.640) = \\boxed{50.2^\\circ}$$\n\n**Part (b): Projection of the force along its own line.**\n\nThe unit vector from Example 5.2 is $\\hat{u}_{AB} = 0.514\\hat{i} + 0.686\\hat{j} - 0.514\\hat{k}$. The axial component is the dot product:\n\n$$F_\\| = \\vec{F} \\cdot \\hat{u}_{AB} = (128.6)(0.514) + (171.5)(0.686) + (-128.6)(-0.514)$$\n\n$$F_\\| = 66.1 + 117.6 + 66.1 = \\boxed{249.8 \\approx 250 \\text{ N}}$$\n\nThis is the expected sanity check: a force projected onto its own line of action recovers the full magnitude. The same machinery, applied to a different member direction, would peel off only the part of the load that runs axially.\n\n---\n\n### Example 5.4: Cross Product for the Moment of a Force\n\n**Problem:** A wrench applies a 500 N force in the +x direction at a point located $\\vec{r} = 0.4\\hat{k}$ m from a bolt at the origin (the handle stands 0.4 m above the bolt along the z axis). Find the moment about the bolt.\n\n**Step 1: Set up the cross product.** With $\\vec{r} = 0\\hat{i} + 0\\hat{j} + 0.4\\hat{k}$ m and $\\vec{F} = 500\\hat{i} + 0\\hat{j} + 0\\hat{k}$ N:\n\n$$\\vec{M}_O = \\vec{r} \\times \\vec{F} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 500 & 0 & 0 \\end{vmatrix}$$\n\n**Step 2: Expand each component.**\n\n$$M_x = (0)(0) - (0.4)(0) = 0$$\n\n$$M_y = -\\big[(0)(0) - (0.4)(500)\\big] = -(-200) = +200$$\n\n$$M_z = (0)(0) - (0)(500) = 0$$\n\n**Step 3: State the moment.**\n\n$$\\vec{M}_O = \\boxed{200\\,\\hat{j} \\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}}$$\n\nThe moment points along +y, perpendicular to both the lever arm and the force, as the right-hand rule predicts. Its magnitude $|\\vec{M}_O| = rF\\sin 90^\\circ = (0.4)(500)(1) = 200$ N$\\cdot$m confirms the cross-product result. Had we written $\\vec{F} \\times \\vec{r}$ by mistake, every sign would flip and the moment would point the wrong way.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Force-vector skill in play |\n| --- | --- |\n| Guy-cable masts and radio towers | 3D unit vectors to express each cable's pull before equilibrium |\n| Truss and frame joints | Dot product for the angle between members; component resolution |\n| Wrenches, gear shafts, lever arms | Cross product for the moment of an applied force |\n| Cable-stayed and suspension bridges | 2D and 3D resolution of stay/hanger forces into deck and tower loads |\n| Robotics and manipulator arms | Dot product for joint-axis projections; cross product for torques |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same resolve-then-sum logic used on a ring bolt with three cables scales directly to a tower held by a dozen guy lines or a bridge deck carried by a hundred hangers. The number of forces changes; the vector bookkeeping does not.\n\n---\n\n## Common Errors and Their Diagnoses\n\n- **Adding magnitudes instead of components.** A 200 N + 100 N force pair almost never produces a 300 N resultant. Always resolve, then sum.\n- **Quadrant errors in $\\tan^{-1}$.** Use both component signs, or use `atan2` if you are computing numerically.\n- **Direction cosines that do not sum in squares to 1.** That is a corruption signal; recompute the unit vector from the position vector.\n- **Applying $\\vec{r} \\times \\vec{F}$ with the wrong $\\vec{r}$.** $\\vec{r}$ must run from the moment point to any point on F's line of action, not the other way around.\n- **Treating dot and cross products interchangeably.** Dot is scalar; cross is vector. The two answer different questions.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why the dot product is the right tool to find the axial (parallel) component of a force on a member, while the cross product is the right tool to find that force's moment about a point. Tie each to its geometric output.\" answer=\"The dot product F . u = F cos(theta) extracts the part of the force that lies ALONG the unit vector u, which is exactly the axial component a member carries. Its output is a single scalar because a projection is just a length along a line, and it vanishes when the force is perpendicular to u. The cross product r x F = rF sin(theta) instead measures the turning effect: it uses the PERPENDICULAR part of the force times the lever arm, and its output is a vector whose direction (by the right-hand rule) gives the axis of rotation. Moments are inherently directional in 3D, so a vector output is required, and the cross product is zero when r and F are parallel because a force aimed straight at the point produces no turning.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| 2D resolution | $F_x = F\\cos\\theta$, $F_y = F\\sin\\theta$ |\n| Resultant from components | $R = \\sqrt{R_x^2 + R_y^2}$, $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(R_y/R_x)$ |\n| 3D unit vector | $\\hat{u}_{AB} = \\vec{r}_{AB}/|\\vec{r}_{AB}|$, $\\vec{F} = F\\,\\hat{u}_{AB}$ |\n| Direction-cosine identity | $\\cos^2\\alpha + \\cos^2\\beta + \\cos^2\\gamma = 1$ |\n| Dot product | $\\vec{A}\\cdot\\vec{B} = A_xB_x + A_yB_y + A_zB_z = AB\\cos\\theta$ |\n| Projection onto a line | $F_\\| = \\vec{F}\\cdot\\hat{u}$ |\n| Cross product (moment) | $\\vec{M}_O = \\vec{r}\\times\\vec{F}$, $|\\vec{M}_O| = rF\\sin\\theta$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- A force is a vector; its direction is as load-bearing as its magnitude.\n- Resolve into components, sum the components, then reassemble. This beats angle-by-angle addition.\n- In 3D, always build the unit vector from a position vector instead of guessing angles.\n- The dot product answers \"what angle?\" and \"how much along this line?\" (scalar).\n- The cross product answers \"what moment?\" and \"which perpendicular direction?\" (vector), and its order matters.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next module we turn the closed force polygon into algebra: applying $\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$ to particles, where the vector skills built here become the equilibrium equations that carry the rest of statics.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"force_vector\",\"force_components\",\"unit_vector\",\"direction_cosines\",\"dot_product\",\"cross_product\"]'></concept-map>\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"3D Force Vectors\" context=\"A radio tower is held by three guy cables anchored at ground points A, B, C and meeting at the tower top T(0, 0, 30) m. Cable tensions are unknown. The student is asked to write each cable's force vector in component form before applying equilibrium.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n### Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Two forces act at a point: F\u2081 = 80 N along the +x axis, and F\u2082 = 60 N at 90\u00b0 above the +x axis (along +y). What is the magnitude of the resultant?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"140 N (sum of magnitudes).\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"100 N (Pythagorean of components: \u221a(80\u00b2 + 60\u00b2) = 100).\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"20 N (difference of magnitudes).\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Perpendicular forces combine as the Pythagorean of their magnitudes: \u221a(80\u00b2 + 60\u00b2) = \u221a10000 = 100 N. Adding magnitudes (140) is the classic scalar mistake.\"></interactive-quiz>\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "force_vec_2d_resultant", "type": "multiple_choice", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_id": "force_components", "concept_ids": ["force_components", "force_vector"], "stem": "Two perpendicular forces act on a particle: F\u2081 = 80 N along the +x axis and F\u2082 = 60 N along the +y axis. What is the magnitude of the resultant?", "options": ["140 N (sum of magnitudes).", "100 N (Pythagorean combination of perpendicular components).", "20 N (difference of magnitudes).", "4800 N (product of magnitudes)."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Perpendicular forces combine as \u221a(80\u00b2 + 60\u00b2) = 100 N. Adding magnitudes gives 140 N, which would only be correct if the forces were collinear and same direction.", "misconception_id": "scalar_addition_of_vectors"}, {"id": "force_vec_3d_unit", "type": "multiple_choice", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_id": "unit_vector", "concept_ids": ["unit_vector", "force_vector", "direction_cosines"], "stem": "A cable runs from A(1, 2, 4) m to B(4, 6, 0) m. What is the unit vector along the line from A toward B?", "options": ["(3i + 4j - 4k) / \u221a41", "(3i + 4j + 4k) / \u221a41 (sign of z component flipped)", "(1i + 2j + 4k) / \u221a21 (the position of A normalized)", "(4i + 6j + 0k) / \u221a52 (the position of B normalized)"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "r_AB = (4-1)i + (6-2)j + (0-4)k = 3i + 4j - 4k, |r_AB| = \u221a(9+16+16) = \u221a41. The unit vector is r_AB / |r_AB| = (3i + 4j - 4k)/\u221a41.", "misconception_id": "unit_vector_from_endpoint"}, {"id": "force_vec_dot_projection", "type": "multiple_choice", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_id": "dot_product", "concept_ids": ["dot_product", "force_vector"], "stem": "Force F = 100i + 0j + 0k N is applied to a member whose axis is along unit vector u = (i + j + k)/\u221a3. What is the component of F along the member's axis?", "options": ["100 N (the full magnitude).", "100/\u221a3 \u2248 57.7 N (F \u00b7 u).", "0 N (perpendicular projection).", "300 N (F times \u221a3)."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The projection along the axis is F \u00b7 u = (100)(1/\u221a3) + 0 + 0 = 100/\u221a3 \u2248 57.7 N.", "misconception_id": "projection_equals_magnitude"}, {"id": "force_vec_cross_anticommute", "type": "multiple_choice", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_id": "cross_product", "concept_ids": ["cross_product"], "stem": "Why does swapping the order of operands in a cross product (A \u00d7 B \u2192 B \u00d7 A) change the sign of the result?", "options": ["It does not; the cross product is commutative like the dot product.", "Because the cross product is anti-commutative: A \u00d7 B = -B \u00d7 A. The right-hand rule reverses direction when the operands swap.", "Because the magnitudes change.", "Because the dot product would be needed instead."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The cross product is anti-commutative. Swapping operands flips the right-hand-rule direction. This is the most common source of moment-sign errors.", "misconception_id": "cross_product_commutative"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "scalar_addition_of_vectors", "pattern": "vector_to_scalar_collapse", "description": "Adding force magnitudes as if they were scalars without resolving into components", "trigger": "learner reports the resultant of two non-collinear forces as the sum of their magnitudes", "feedback": "Forces have direction. Add them by components or by the parallelogram rule. Magnitudes only sum when the forces are collinear and in the same direction.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "unit_vector_from_endpoint", "pattern": "vector_origin_error", "description": "Building the unit vector along a line by normalizing one endpoint's position vector instead of the line's position vector", "trigger": "learner divides one endpoint coordinates by their magnitude to obtain a 'unit vector along the line'", "feedback": "The unit vector along a line from A to B uses r_AB = B - A, not A or B alone. The line is the difference of the two position vectors.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "projection_equals_magnitude", "pattern": "dot_product_misuse", "description": "Treating the projection of a force onto an axis as the full force magnitude regardless of angle", "trigger": "learner reports F\u00b7u = |F| even when F and u are not parallel", "feedback": "F \u00b7 u = |F||u|cos\u03b8. Only when F is parallel to u (\u03b8 = 0) does the projection equal |F|. At other angles the projection is smaller.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "cross_product_commutative", "pattern": "operator_property_confusion", "description": "Treating the cross product as commutative like the dot product", "trigger": "learner writes M = F \u00d7 r instead of M = r \u00d7 F (or vice versa) without noting the sign change", "feedback": "The cross product is anti-commutative: A \u00d7 B = -B \u00d7 A. For moments use M = r \u00d7 F where r runs from the moment point to any point on F's line of action.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}